```q
// @overview
// Return true if a given number is prime, and false otherwise.
//
// @param x {int} Input number.
//
// @return {boolean} 1b if a given number is prime, and 0b otherwise
is_prime:{[x]
    // Numbers <= 1 are not prime
    if[x<=1; return 0b];

    // 2 is the only even prime
    if[x=2; return 1b];

    // Even numbers > 2 are not prime
    if[not 0=x mod 2; return 1b];
    if[0=x mod 2; return 0b];

    // Check odd divisors up to sqrt(x)
    // We only need to check up to the integer square root of x
    limit: floor sqrt x;

    // Generate odd numbers from 3 to limit
    candidates: 3+2*til (limit-3)%2+1;

    // Check if any candidate divides x evenly
    // If none do, x is prime
    all[not 0=x mod candidates]
    }
```

This implementation:
- Handles edge cases (numbers ≤ 1, 2, even numbers)
- Only checks odd divisors up to √x (optimization)
- Uses idiomatic q constructs (`til`, `mod`, `all`, `not`)
- Returns boolean as specified (`1b`/`0b`)

