Specifically, in the expansion of $n! \times (n-1)! \times \dots \times 1!$:
- The number $1$ appears in every factorial term ($n$ times).
- The number $2$ appears in every factorial term from $2!$ upwards ($n-1$ times).
- The number $k$ appears in every factorial term from $k!$ upwards ($n - k + 1$ times).

Thus, `brazilian_factorial(n)` is equivalent to $\prod_{k=1}^{n} k^{n-k+1}$.

